(Topological Rudin's Lemma)\cite{heckmann2013quasicontinuous}
	Let $X$ be a topological space and $\mathcal{A}$ an irreducible subset of the
	Smyth power space $Q(X)$. Then every closed set $F\subseteq X$ that meets all members of $\mathcal{A}$ contains a minimal irreducible closed subset $C$ that still meets all members of $\mathcal{A}$.

\cite{gierz2003continuous}
	For two spaces $X$ and $Y$, let $H$ be a Scott open set of $\mathcal{O}(X)$, $V$ be an open set of $Y$ and set $$N(H\leftarrow V)=\{f\in TOP(X,Y): f^{-1}(V)\in H\}.$$
	The Isbell topology on $TOP(X,Y)$ is generated by $\{N(H\leftarrow V)\mid H\in\sigma(\mathcal{O}(X)), V\in\mathcal{O}(Y)\}$ as a subbase and this function space is denoted by $[X,Y]$.

\cite{gierz2003continuous}
	A full subcategory $\mathcal{K}$ of $TOP_0$ is reflective if for each $T_0$ space $X$, there exists $KX\in\mathcal{K}$ (the $K$-completion for $X$) and a continuous mapping $\mu_X: X\to KX$ (the $K$-reflection for $X$) satisfying
	the universal property: for any continuous mapping $f: X\to Z$ to a space $Z\in\mathcal{K}$, there exists a unique continuous mapping $g: KX\to Z$ such that $g\circ\mu_X=f$.
